  "plain_language": {
    "summary": "ON THIS MODEL AND THESE 154 QUESTIONS, handing the match decision to the byte route does not separate 'this was written' from 'this was never written'.  The literal sentence that was stored finds itself every time (22/22), but that is the trivial case.  When the question is the same record asked in a different word position, the byte route picks the shared template record, not the record: 0/22 correct, and 0/66 on the never-written wordings.  A never-written word at the same position gets exactly the same alignment score as the legitimate paraphrase, so a threshold cannot tell them apart.  Today's averaged-pooled address does better on the same questions (14/66 on the never-written wordings), because pooling throws the position away and keeps the novel word, which is exactly the information the byte route discards.  The copy gate is not modified and still reads the old mean key, so it still refuses all 22 faces; the byte address never reaches it.",
    "counting_rule": "the never-written number counts a hit only when the winning line is that record's own slot.  The older convention (the never-written wording lands on the same slot as the read wording) is reported too: arm A 35/66, arm B 66/66.  The older convention is degenerate for the byte arm: everything lands on the same template record, so everything 'agrees'.",
    "what_it_shows": "the primitive is not broken and it runs with zero training; what it measures is byte identity over a fixed template, not content identity.  On a battery where every sentence shares one template and differs by one word, byte identity cannot be discriminative: it answers 'is this the same string' and the question is 'is this the same fact'.",
    "what_it_does_not_show": "it does not show that byte matching is useless in general -- the 19.2r gate used it with an injected alignment offset on a task where the object description was a verbatim substring.  It does not show that the model cannot learn, and it does not test growth, capacity or training.",
    "next": "the verdict is alignment_not_discriminative; no encoding, geometry or threshold tuning follows from this run."
  },
